WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:01.734
A module is one of the simplest

00:00:01.747 --> 00:00:05.263
ways to organize Python code In practical

00:00:05.289 --> 00:00:07.487
terms a module is usually a Python

00:00:07.513 --> 00:00:09.447
file with a dot P Y extension

00:00:10.382 --> 00:00:13.513
That file can contain functions variables constants

00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:15.711
or classes that another Python file can

00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:19.066
use Python also comes with many built-in

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:22.697
modules One example is the math module

00:00:23.632 --> 00:00:26.000
If we write import math Python makes

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:28.289
the tools inside the math module available

00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:31.487
to our program After importing the module

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:33.750
we can access its contents by using

00:00:33.776 --> 00:00:35.829
the module name followed by a dot

00:00:36.763 --> 00:00:39.105
For example math dot square root gives

00:00:39.132 --> 00:00:40.921
us access to the square root function

00:00:40.947 --> 00:00:43.895
stored inside the math module If we

00:00:43.921 --> 00:00:46.289
calculate math dot square root of eighty-one

00:00:46.487 --> 00:00:49.276
the result is nine A useful way

00:00:49.303 --> 00:00:50.855
to think about a module is as

00:00:50.882 --> 00:00:54.118
a toolbox The module is the toolbox

00:00:54.342 --> 00:00:55.934
and the functions inside it are the

00:00:55.961 --> 00:00:59.303
tools Importing the module is like bringing

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:02.579
the toolbox into your workspace Then you

00:01:02.605 --> 00:01:05.500
choose the tool you need This approach

00:01:05.513 --> 00:01:07.066
is useful because we do not need

00:01:07.079 --> 00:01:11.237
to rewrite common functionality Python already
provides

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.197
modules for mathematics random values dates and

00:01:14.224 --> 00:01:17.171
times statistics file handling and many other

00:01:17.197 --> 00:01:20.500
tasks Later we will also create our

00:01:20.526 --> 00:01:25.079
own module The same idea applies We place

00:01:25.105 --> 00:01:27.487
reusable code in a separate Python file

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.960
and then import that file when we

00:01:28.974 --> 00:01:29.895
need its functions
